sections.contentEn:
The hallmark of the limited liability company is the doctrine of separate legal personality (persona standi in judicio), which establishes that corporate liabilities belong strictly to the entity. Under Article 3 Paragraph (1) of UU PT, shareholders are not personally liable for agreements entered into on behalf of the company and do not bear corporate losses exceeding the nominal value of their subscribed shares.

However, this statutory veil is not absolute. Under Article 3 Paragraph (2) of UU PT, the doctrine of Piercing the Corporate Veil applies, eliminating limited liability and exposing shareholders to joint and several personal liability across four statutory exceptions:
1. Non-Fulfillment of Legal Status: The substantive and formal statutory requirements of the company as a legal entity have not yet been completed (e.g., operating prior to ministerial ratification without fulfilling post-incorporation formalities);
2. Alter Ego and Bad Faith: The shareholder, either directly or indirectly, utilizes the corporate structure in bad faith solely for personal enrichment or fraudulent purposes;
3. Unlawful Acts (Onrechtmatige Daad): The shareholder is implicated in an unlawful act committed by or through the instrumentality of the corporation;
4. Unlawful Commingling or Depletion of Corporate Assets: The shareholder unlawfully utilizes corporate assets without fair consideration, resulting directly in the company's financial inability to discharge its liabilities to third-party creditors.

Furthermore, under Article 104 and Article 115 of UU PT, if bankruptcy occurs due to the fault or negligence of the Board of Directors or Board of Commissioners and the liquidation assets are insufficient to satisfy all creditor claims, members of the board bear joint and several personal liability for the outstanding deficiency.

sections.contentId:
Karakteristik fundamental dari perseroan terbatas adalah doktrin badan hukum mandiri (separate legal personality) dan pemisahan harta kekayaan. Pasal 3 ayat (1) UUPT menegaskan bahwa pemegang saham perseroan tidak bertanggung jawab secara pribadi atas perikatan yang dibuat atas nama perseroan dan tidak bertanggung jawab atas kerugian perseroan melebihi saham yang dimiliki.

Kendati demikian, perlindungan tabir perseroan tersebut dapat disingkap melalui doktrin Piercing the Corporate Veil sebagaimana diatur dalam Pasal 3 ayat (2) UUPT, yang menjadikan pemegang saham memikul tanggung jawab pribadi tanpa batas (unlimited personal liability) apabila terjadi salah satu dari 4 kondisi berikut:
1. Persyaratan Formal Belum Terpenuhi: Persyaratan perseroan sebagai badan hukum belum atau tidak terpenuhi secara sah;
2. Itikad Buruk / Alter Ego: Pemegang saham yang bersangkutan baik langsung maupun tidak langsung dengan iktikad buruk memanfaatkan perseroan semata-mata untuk kepentingan pribadi;
3. Keterlibatan Perbuatan Melawan Hukum: Pemegang saham yang bersangkutan terlibat dalam perbuatan melawan hukum (onrechtmatige daad) yang dilakukan oleh perseroan;
4. Penyelewengan Kekayaan Perseroan: Pemegang saham baik langsung maupun tidak langsung secara melawan hukum menggunakan kekayaan perseroan, yang mengakibatkan kekayaan perseroan menjadi tidak cukup untuk melunasi utang perseroan kepada para kreditor.

Selain itu, berdasarkan Pasal 104 dan Pasal 115 UUPT, apabila terjadi kepailitan akibat kesalahan atau kelalaian Direksi maupun Dewan Komisaris dan harta pailit perseroan tidak cukup melunasi seluruh kewajiban, maka anggota Direksi dan Dewan Komisaris secara tanggung renteng bertanggung jawab secara pribadi atas seluruh sisa utang yang belum terbayar.

sections.contentEn:
Major structural adjustments and corporate reorganizations are strictly regulated under Chapter IX (Articles 122 through 137) of UU PT to protect the interests of creditors, minority shareholders, employees, and fair competition:

1. Mergers (Penggabungan / Amalgamation): One or more companies merge into an existing surviving entity. The transferring companies dissolve by operation of law without going through statutory liquidation, and all assets, liabilities, and rights vest automatically in the surviving corporation.
2. Consolidations (Peleburan / Consolidation): Two or more companies consolidate to establish a completely new corporate entity. All consolidating companies dissolve without liquidation, with universal legal succession to the newly incorporated entity.
3. Acquisitions (Pengambilalihan / Takeovers): Legal transaction acquiring equity or voting shares resulting in a change of corporate control (pengendalian perseroan), executed either through the Board of Directors or directly from existing shareholders.
4. Demergers / Spin-offs (Pemisahan): Corporate divisions categorized into Pure Demergers (Pemisahan Murni, where the original company dissolves and splits all assets and liabilities between two or more new entities) or Incomplete Demergers (Pemisahan Tidak Murni / Spin-off, where the existing company continues to exist while transferring a portion of its assets and liabilities to another entity).

Mandatory Procedural Sequence:
- Joint Proposal: Directors must draft a comprehensive Corporate Action Plan approved by the Board of Commissioners;
- Public & Employee Notification: Summary of the plan must be published in at least one national Indonesian newspaper and delivered in writing to all employees at least thirty (30) days prior to the GMS summons (Article 127);
- Creditor Protections: Creditors possess the statutory right to submit formal objections within 14 days of publication. Restructuring cannot proceed to ministerial approval until all creditor claims are settled or guaranteed;
- GMS Approval: Supermajority quorum of 3/4 voting attendance and 3/4 affirmative approval;
- Deed Execution & Approval: Formalized by notarial deed in Indonesian and submitted to the Minister of Law and Human Rights for approval or receipt of notification.

